\ Regression test: a curried closure resolves its creation-site bare words at every consumer
\ shape: a direct call, a spawned task, and an iterator stored across statements.
\
\ A body its closure owns never enters the pointer-keyed resolution maps, so each consumer must
\ carry the closure to the execution entry point. The stored iterator is the long-lived shape: it
\ holds the closure beside the body and hands it back at each element, well after the statement
\ that built it has finished.

use "testing" ;
use { make-secret-caller: make-secret-method: } "./closure_carrier_holder.1z" ;

\ Stored first, drained last, so the closure rides the iterator across the other two sections.
held: { 1 2 3 } >iterator make-secret-method #map ;

"direct call" test-section

make-secret-caller call
"LIB-SECRET" "a curried closure resolves its module-private word at a direct call" assert=

"spawned task" test-section

[
  out: 1 <buffered-channel> ;
  parent-made: make-secret-caller ;
  [ parent-made out send ] spawn drop
  out receive
  "LIB-SECRET" "a closure created before the spawn resolves inside the task" assert=
] task-scope

"stored iterator" test-section

held #collect
{ "LIB-SECRET" "LIB-SECRET" "LIB-SECRET" }
"a stored iterator hands the closure back at each element" assert=
